3c589.device 1.5auto3 - picks 10BASE-T or BNC by itself
========================================================

This is Neil Cafferkey's 3c589.device 1.5 (Aminet driver/net/3c589.lha,
GPL) with one change: when the card is opened, the driver looks for a
10BASE-T link and uses the BNC (coax) port when there is none.

Why: 3Com 3C589 cards come with the EEPROM set to "10BASE-T with
autoselect". Version 1.5 reads the link-beat bit once, right after
power-on, when it does not yet mean anything, and then falls back to
10BASE-T. On a BNC network the card stays silent: no packets go out,
none come in, and only the dongle's LED tells that something is wrong
(it stays off). The Windows driver gets around this with its
"Transceiver type" setting, which lives in the Windows registry, not
on the card.

What 1.5auto3 does when the card is opened (DECnet-Start, or when a
TCP/IP stack goes online):

  1. selects 10BASE-T with link-beat detection,
  2. ignores the link-beat bit for 0.3 seconds (it is still set from
     power-on for about 0.1 seconds),
  3. takes 10BASE-T if the bit then stays set for 0.3 seconds; a
     switch port may need about one second before it gives link
     pulses,
  4. otherwise, after 3 seconds, takes BNC.

So opening the card takes up to 3 seconds longer on BNC. The driver
waits in a loop while multitasking is held (Forbid), so the whole
system stands still for that time (the Workbench clock stops, the
mouse pointer may too). This happens only when the card is opened by the
first program (at boot, or DECnet-Start with no TCP/IP stack running),
not when a second program opens it. On 10BASE-T the pause is shorter,
about one second. No data is lost. The choice is
made only when the card is opened: after changing the cable, stop and
start the network software (for DECnet: DECnet-Stop, DECnet-Start).
A card whose EEPROM is set to a fixed port (autoselect off) keeps that
port, as before. The driver never writes to the card's EEPROM.

Tested on a real A1200 (OS 3.2.3) with DECnet: 3C589D (two cards,
dongle with BNC and RJ45) and 3C589B (10BASE-T cable and two BNC
dongles), each on BNC and on 10BASE-T.


Installing
----------
Install 3c589.device 1.5 from Aminet first (it brings pccard.library
and the installer). Then copy Devs/networks/3c589.device from this
archive over DEVS:networks/3c589.device. Keep the file name: the
driver only opens under the name 3c589.device (another name gives
error -1).

In the A1200's PCMCIA slot the card usually needs CardReset (Aminet
util/boot/CardReset.lha) before it can be opened, even after power-on.


Contents
--------
  Devs/networks/3c589.device   the driver, 1.5auto3 (14.11.26)
  1.5auto3.patch               changes against the 1.5 source
  Source/                      the complete source of this version
  GPL                          the licence (GNU General Public Licence)

The patch also has small changes that let the 1.5 source build with
today's amiga-gcc (bebbo, gcc 6.5); Source/inc/ holds the pccard.library
headers and short header shims for that. Build, in Source/:

  CF="-m68000 -msmall-code -O2 -w -fno-toplevel-reorder -noixemul -Iinc"
  for f in device unit request; do m68k-amigaos-gcc $CF -c $f.c; done
  m68k-amigaos-gcc -m68000 -noixemul -nostartfiles -nostdlib -s \
     device.o unit.o request.o -lamiga -lgcc -o 3c589.device

